I have a question about the method in which the record file is updated
and displayed when the toptenwin option is set on.
Specifically, why is the scoreboard displayed to the user while it is
still be written back into the record file?


My question stems from a situation I've run into on a telnet server
(dgamelaunch on LINUX) I'm running that leads me to believe this
behavior is a vulnerability/oversight. Specifically, if some malicious
or unknowing user has the toptenwin option set (which is not uncommon
if they'd like to see where they placed on the scores after their game)
and will be shown too many scores to fit in one terminal height,
Nethack will pause with the standard -MORE- at the bottom of the
screen. If the user were then to sit staring at the screen for a while,
or worse yet were to disconnect from the server and so terminate their
process, the record file will be left possibly empty, corrupt, or
complete (depending on how far along the writeback was when the -MORE-
was required) but in any event the record_lock file will not be removed
(the unlock call never happens) and no further scores will be submitted
to record until the problem is manually fixed by an admin.

Given that unpleasant situation happening a few times and me
experiencing all three possible record file outcomes, I looked into the
source and so far I cannot see why display of the scores must happen
simultaneously with the write out to the file. However, I bow to the
combined prowess of the RGRN, and so


does anyone know a reason why the display of the scoreboard sh/could
not be done after record is reconstructed and unlocked??


